stakeholder_update = f'''<section id="director-review"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Vini and MB</h2></div><div class="two-col"><article><h3>Vini: audit beneficiary and overlapping signer</h3><p>Vini proposed A249’s 16,900-USDC audit payment to Vini’s address and supplied one of its three deciding YES votes. The alleged earlier recusal agreement remains unverified; audit scope and independent acceptance still need review.</p><p>Vini’s board address is also a 10-weight ERIS signer. Vini rejected the earlier Estima A258 and supported the later two-contract migration-admin transfer to the Phoenix board. October’s new Stewardship schedules omitted Vini while separate infrastructure payments continued; the reason is unresolved.</p></article><article><h3>MB: voting alignment and project relationships</h3><p>MB voted YES on 251 of 252 proposals authored by 0xphilipp where MB cast a ballot. Many were routine operations; this is a reason to examine independent review, not proof of coordination.</p><p>The completed Treasury capture records 57,096 USDC to MB in its 2026 window. Two close-together claims represented separate entitlements. AADAO names MB as Superbolt’s representative; the full grant-to-distribution and delivery reconciliation remains open.</p></article></div><p class="note">Monitored Vini and MB wallet histories remain truncated. These records do not establish lifetime income or the absence of other relationships. Other directors approved Estima’s original A266 grant without 0xphilipp; A272’s proposed removal and threshold reduction failed.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/phoenix/director_profiles.json', 'Director voting and scoped payment profiles'), ('/record/audit/phoenix/key_proposals.json', 'A249, Estima and A272 proposal records'), (FUNDING_REPORT, 'AADAO project funding and acceptance report')])}</section>
<section id="funded-locks"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Public funding also created voting locks</h2><p>On 5 November 2024, A43 created four permanent TLA locks funded with 83,000 ampLUNA in total.</p></div>{table(['Recipient role', 'Lock ID', 'ampLUNA funded', 'A43 ballot'], lock_rows, 'Original creation and ballots; later ownership and lock terms require separate history checks.')}<p>Three recipient wallets supplied the passing 75/100 YES weight. Phoenix A33 separately created four permanent locks with {number(supplement['phoenix_A33_locks']['facts']['total_ampLUNA'], 6)} ampLUNA in total. This is Phoenix A33, not Lion DAO A33.</p><p class="note">These are TLA voting locks, not native LUNA ballots. Current queries show fixed end periods for locks 429 and 432, with an additional 1 ampLUNA in 432. The change transactions and compliance with award terms remain unresolved; a changed state alone does not establish a breach.</p>{source_list([(LEADS + 'voting_locks.json', 'Funded locks with owners and event references'), (LEADS + 'queries/phoenix_A43_votes.json', 'A43 original ballot response'), (LEADS + 'verified_findings.json', 'Findings, current lock states and limitations')])}</section>
<section id="superbolt"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Superbolt: three beneficiary YES votes</h2></div><p>Neutron proposal 35 executed a 90,000-USDC batch on 5 August 2025: one 45,000-USDC payment and three 15,000-USDC payments. Its three recorded YES ballots came from three recipient wallets. This was collective approval, not a unilateral payment.</p><p>Separately, AADAO reports 80,000 dollars paid from an original 125,000-dollar Superbolt award, with 45,000 cancelled after rescoping. That upstream award has not been fully reconciled to the distribution. They are not additive funding, and neither amount is established as personal profit.</p>{source_list([(LEADS + 'queries/superbolt_35_proposal.json', 'Distribution proposal'), (LEADS + 'queries/superbolt_35_votes.json', 'All three recorded YES ballots'), (LEADS + 'verified_findings.json', 'Execution transfers and denomination check'), (FUNDING_REPORT, 'External award, rescoping and payment status')])}</section>'''
creda_approval = f'''<section id="creda-approval"><div class="section-heading"><h2>ERIS/Creda’s 210,000-USDC grant</h2><p>Treasury action 72 records a 210,000-USDC grant to the published ERIS address. All four milestones are marked claimed.</p></div>{table(['Approval', 'Milestones enabled', 'USDC', 'YES votes'], [['A209', 'Kickoff: 40,000; mainnet launch: 100,000; eMode + isolation: 60,000', '200,000', '0xphilipp, Vini, MB'], ['A215', 'Analytics', '10,000', '0xphilipp, Vini, MB']])}<p>0xphilipp proposed both releases and supplied one of the three deciding YES votes on each. A director associated with the funded project took part in enabling its grant payments. The records show collective approval, not unilateral control.</p><p class="note">These proposals enabled milestones within an existing grant; they were not two new grants. Claimed status is recorded by the Treasury contract. Independent acceptance of every deliverable and personal profit are not established. Vini’s separate 16,900-USDC audit payment appears in the director review below.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/phoenix/key_proposals.json', 'A209 and A215: messages and all recorded ballots'), (LEADS + 'queries/review_treasury_action_72.json', 'Treasury action 72: recipient, amounts and claimed status'), ('/treasury/#project-funding', 'Other project grants and external support')])}</section>'''
stakeholders = stakeholders.replace('<section id="board"', creda_approval + '<section id="board"', 1)
stakeholders = insert_before_reading(stakeholders, stakeholder_update)
stakeholders = insert_before_reading(stakeholders, '<div class="context-note"><strong>About wallet labels.</strong> Public handles, roles and addresses are used only where the reviewed records support them. Address-level relationships do not establish exclusive custody, common ownership or intent.</div>')
page('Stakeholders and interests', stakeholders, '/stakeholders/')

# Treasury and forensic accounting.
treasury = head('Treasury and counterparties', 'Grants, compensation, asset trades and payments requiring further records.') + f'''<div class="wrap page-content"><section class="section-grid"><div class="section-intro"><h2>What the payments represent</h2></div><div><p>The reviewed records mix grants, scheduled compensation, reimbursements, protocol operations, locked allocations and asset exchanges. Each needs different evidence. A large LUNA receipt may be an OTC trade; a contract receipt may be liquidity deployment; a scheduled claim may relate to an earlier entitlement period.</p><p>Do not add upstream grants and downstream distributions twice. Do not describe recurring transfers as vote buying without an agreement, communications or other direct evidence.</p></div></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Recurring validator-to-ERIS transfers</h2><p>Twenty transfers from February through October 2026; ten from each validator account. No memo establishes their purpose.</p></div>{table(['Sender', 'Transfers', 'Total', 'What is missing'], [['Galactic Punks', '10', '116,735 LUNA', 'Agreement, invoice, pricing formula and disclosure history'], ['Lion DAO', '10', '105,227 LUNA', 'Agreement, invoice, pricing formula and disclosure history'], ['<strong>Total</strong>', '<strong>20</strong>', '<strong>221,962 LUNA</strong>', 'Classification remains unresolved']])}<p>The purpose of these transfers is unresolved. Service terms are needed to classify them and establish whether the arrangement predates 4853.</p>{source_list([('/record/audit/payment_summary.json', 'Download the payment summary and all 20 transfer locators'), ('/record/audit/transfers/monthly_validator_to_eris/', 'Open the preserved original transaction directory')])}</section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Recurring payments beyond the board</h2><p>These recipients require the same invoice, entitlement-period and deliverable reconciliation as directors.</p></div>{table(['Recipient', 'Observed pattern', 'Review question'], [['<code>terra1azcp…j2tz2</code>', '65,000 USDC across 13 payments of 5,000', 'Core-work agreement and acceptance'], ['<code>terra12jrv…g7egn</code>', '32,500 USDC across 11 payments, primarily 3,000', 'Comms agreement and outputs'], ['<code>terra1ez76…clsxu</code>', '14,500 USDC across ten payments, primarily 1,500', 'Ambassador scope and outputs'], ['<code>terra1lqqs…meqs</code>', 'Recurring 1,800-USDC moderator schedules', 'Coverage period and approval'], ['<code>terra1xtyq…vy68</code>', 'Recurring 1,500-USDC moderator schedules', 'Coverage period and approval']])}</section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Director compensation and October schedules</h2></div><p>The 2026 coverage window records 57,096 USDC to MB, 66,480 USDC to Vini and 46,745.70 USDC to Rose under the report’s classifications. Vini’s total includes a 16,900-USDC audit payment and recurring infra payments. Rose separately received 237,323.047934 LUNA in an OTC trade supplying approximately 0.19657723 WBTC; that is not compensation.</p><p>October actions 118–120 created new Stewardship payments for 0xphilipp, Rose and MB, but not Vini. Separate infra actions continued. The chain record does not explain the omission.</p></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>OTC and related-party execution</h2></div><p>The 50-weight ERIS signer received 795,991.566014 LUNA through Treasury offers 91, 106 and 111. The checked messages are <code>execute_otc</code>, so these are asset exchanges, not salary. The governance questions are price fairness, approval, disclosure and execution quality.</p><p>Other large OTC recipients must likewise be valued using the asset they supplied and execution-time prices. LUNA totals alone do not establish expense, gain or loss.</p></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Records needed to reconcile payments</h2></div><ol class="request-list"><li>Complete action exports: recipient, entitlement period, approval, claim, cancellation and transaction.</li><li>Contracts, invoices and pricing for monthly validator transfers and the ERIS invoice to Rebel.</li><li>Statements of work, competing quotes where relevant, measurable deliverables and independent acceptance.</li><li>Related-party OTC terms, supplied assets, market-rate checks and execution timestamps.</li><li>Beneficiary/decider overlap and recusal records for A159, A249, A266 and A271.</li><li>Admin and owner powers over recipients, schedules, fees, code and future voting.</li><li>Account continuity without assuming exclusive key custody; reconcile current and predecessor role wallets.</li><li>Direct wallet-flow graphs that distinguish native transfers, CW20 transfers, contract operations and module accounts.</li></ol></section>{nextlinks(('/stakeholders/', 'Back: stakeholders'), ('/governance/', 'Next: governance controls ' + ARROW))}</div>'''
funding_rows = [
    ['Creda', '210,000 USDC', 'Action 72; all four milestones marked claimed', 'No additional external grant verified here'],
    ['Votion', '50,000 USDC', 'Action 28; both milestones marked claimed; February 2025 award', '$50,000 for separate Hydro scope; funder reports payment and completion'],
    ['USDC migration app', '5,000 USDC', 'Action 109; completed funding action', 'No separate external development grant verified here'],
    ['Migration liquidity', '100,000 USDC', 'Action 110; liquidity capital, separate from developer compensation', 'No separate external contribution verified here'],
]
market = year_review['current_creda_metrics']
shares = year_review['approximate_share_supplied_percent']
funding_update = f'''<section id="project-funding"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Who funded the reported work?</h2><p>ERIS’s 2025–2026 retrospective describes its products and operational work. The records identify the following Phoenix grants and external support.</p></div>{table(['Work', 'Phoenix Treasury support', 'Evidence and timing', 'External funding identified'], funding_rows, 'Identified project support, checked 27 October 2026; not a complete ERIS budget.')}<p>The identified direct project grants total <strong>{number(year_review['identified_named_project_grants_USDC'])} USDC</strong>. Votion’s earlier Phoenix award predates the article’s October 2025–October 2026 period; excluding it leaves <strong>{number(year_review['review_period_grants_excluding_earlier_votion_USDC'])} USDC</strong> in grants initiated within that period. The 100,000-USDC liquidity allocation is separate capital, not another development fee.</p><p>Recurring Stewardship compensation is additional, but the record does not allocate it across each listed app or security task. Protocol fees and validator receipts also exist; their contribution to these development costs has not been reconciled.</p><p class="note">These amounts are project funding, not personal profit. The Hydro grant covers separate work, so the two awards do not establish duplicate billing. The records do not support a reliable Treasury-versus-external percentage for all the work in the article.</p>{source_list([(LEADS + 'year_review_analysis.json', 'Funding calculations and source records'), (LEADS + 'queries/review_treasury_action_72.json', 'Creda grant milestones'), (LEADS + 'queries/review_treasury_action_28.json', 'Earlier Votion grant'), (FUNDING_REPORT, 'AADAO: Votion paid and jointly verified complete'), (YEAR_ARTICLE, 'ERIS retrospective being assessed')])}</section>
<section id="creda-outcomes"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Creda deposits and borrowing</h2><p>Contract balances queried on 27 October are shown below. They do not establish performance throughout the year.</p></div>{table(['Metric', 'Captured value', 'Interpretation'], [['Supplied assets', '$' + number(market['total_supplied_usd']), 'Current contract-reported supply'], ['Borrowed assets', '$' + number(market['total_borrowed_usd']), 'Borrowing activity; not another pool of deposits'], ['Designated collateral', '$' + number(market['total_collateral_usd']), 'Overlaps supplied balances; do not add again'], ['Operations and board positions', number(shares['phoenix_operations_and_board'], 1) + '% of supply', 'Public Phoenix contract positions'], ['TLA single-pool position', number(shares['tla_single_pool'], 1) + '% of supply', 'Pooled user deposits; not established as entirely Treasury-funded']])}<p>The article’s current $1M TVL claim is not reproduced by the roughly $553,000 supply query or the market page. A different dated peak or TVL definition could explain it; the comparison does not establish that Creda never reached $1M.</p><p>Combined, the reviewed public-contract positions represent approximately 42% of supplied value. The remaining balance is not proven to be independent external demand. Queries were sequential, without one confirmed common block height, so the percentages are approximate.</p><p>At capture, the LUNA market had approximately 73.85% utilization and an 18.46% annual borrow rate. This shows borrowing at that time; it does not show demand throughout the year, the number of independent borrowers or whether development spending was justified.</p>{details('Delivery and security claims', '<p>The 10,000-USDC Creda analytics payment is verified, but payment is not independent milestone acceptance. A dated unfinished page would not establish that no analytics work existed. AADAO separately reports Votion’s Hydro milestones complete and jointly verified.</p><p>Zero historical bad debt and every borrower remaining healthy throughout the year were not established by the aggregate query. Public audit reports exist, but coverage must be matched to each deployed revision.</p><p>The article’s approximately $10M security attribution was not independently substantiated as a dated dollar calculation. Cosmos Labs documents collective recovery of already-stolen assets and losses on the Hub side; that does not disprove contributions elsewhere. Prevented theft, recovery custody and final restitution are different outcomes.</p>')}{source_list([(LEADS + 'year_review_analysis.json', 'Dated metrics, positions and arithmetic'), (LEADS + 'queries/review_creda_metrics.json', 'Exact metrics response'), ('https://creda.finance/markets', 'Creda market page; current state may differ'), ('https://forum.cosmos.network/t/neutron-governance-attack-cosmos-hub-response-and-recovery-update/17369', 'Cosmos Labs recovery account'), ('https://github.com/SCV-Security/PublicReports/tree/main/Eris%20Protocol', 'Published auditor reports')])}</section>'''
treasury = treasury.replace('<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Recurring validator-to-ERIS transfers', funding_update + '<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Recurring validator-to-ERIS transfers', 1)
page('Treasury and counterparties', treasury, '/treasury/')

# Governance findings, verified handover and longer-term reforms.
governance = head('Board rules and contract control', 'The board received upgrade control over two contracts, but several internal permissions remained unchanged. The records below separate those powers and examine the board’s approval and veto rules.') + f'''<div class="wrap page-content"><section id="board-approvals"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Board approval rules</h2></div><p>The four-seat board requires three YES votes. That means any three can approve an action over the fourth seat’s objection or absence. A159, A249 and A271 show why beneficiary disclosure and recusal matter across the whole board.</p><p>A272 proposed removing Vini and reducing the threshold to 60%. It failed: 0xphilipp and MB voted YES, Rose voted NO and Vini was absent. The current captured board remains four seats at 75%. Sequence alone does not establish motive.</p></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>The Think Tank cast no YES or NO votes on four veto proposals</h2></div><p>Four substantive veto proposals had zero YES and zero NO ballots. Only limited abstentions were recorded. The technical member proposed all five and abstained on all five. P5’s empty message means the proposed veto could not execute even if approved.</p><p>The records leave two problems to resolve: missing votes and an empty veto message. Membership history, notifications and proposal construction need checking; the reasons for non-participation are unknown.</p></section>
<section id="handover"><div class="section-heading"><h2>“Update admin” is not the same as “transfer ownership”</h2><p>The board received upgrade control over two contracts. Their internal control settings were unchanged.</p></div>{table(['Contract / role', 'After ERIS proposal 9', 'Still retained elsewhere'], [['ampLUNA hub CosmWasm admin', 'Phoenix board <code>terra1k8ug…lppjg</code>', '—'], ['ampLUNA hub <code>config.owner</code>', 'Unchanged', '<code>terra1kefa…klgzl</code>; <code>new_owner: null</code>'], ['CW20-ICS20 CosmWasm admin', 'Phoenix board <code>terra1k8ug…lppjg</code>', '—'], ['CW20-ICS20 <code>gov_contract</code>', 'Unchanged', '<code>terra1q0v…tew</code>'], ['Treasury and TLA registry migration admin', 'Not changed by proposal 9', '<code>terra1q0v…tew</code>'], ['TLA registry configuration owner', 'Not changed by proposal 9', '<code>terra1kefa…klgzl</code>; no pending owner']])}<p>Proposal 9 contains exactly two <code>wasm.update_admin</code> messages. Calling it “Transfer Ownership” is therefore incomplete: it transferred code-upgrade control, not all operational/configuration control.</p>{source_list([('https://chainsco.pe/terra2/tx/86E599263604B396BFB0F35F2F4E505E2EA758B024315B1C055C14994E6242D9', 'Proposal 9 execution transaction'), (LCD + '/cosmwasm/wasm/v1/contract/terra10788fkzah89xrdm27zkj5yvhj9x3494lxawzm5qq3vvxcqz2yzaqyd3enk/smart/eyJjb25maWciOnt9fQ==', 'Current hub internal config'), (LCD + '/cosmwasm/wasm/v1/contract/terra1e0mrzy8077druuu42vs0hu7ugguade0cj65dgtauyaw4gsl4kv0qtdf2au/smart/eyJjb25maWciOnt9fQ==', 'Current ICS20 internal config'), ('/record/audit/admin_handover/', 'Download the contract metadata and config captures')])}<h3>Original handover commitments</h3><p>The 2024 TLA launch text says registry ownership transfers after three months and contract admin after one month. The packaged ledger identifies 31 privileged operations in 19 transactions after its conservative cutoff, affecting seven original contracts. The original commitment and current roles were independently checked; every historical ledger operation was not replayed in this review.</p><p class="note">Successful transactions prove use of an address’s authority, not exclusive human key custody. No approved extension was found in the reviewed records; an agreement elsewhere remains possible.</p></section>
<section id="reforms"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Longer-term reforms</h2></div><div class="reform-grid"><article><h3>Disclose and recuse</h3><p>Maintain a current stakeholder register covering grants, compensation, OTC, hosting, delegations and voting power. Require recusal and independent approval for related-party decisions, with the rationale published.</p></article><article><h3>Make veto proposals usable</h3><p>Give the Think Tank reliable notifications, complete executable messages, adequate voting windows and published rationales. Publish participation records and investigate failed veto proposals.</p></article><article><h3>Separate technical powers</h3><p>Document migration admin, internal owner, treasury control and vote operator separately. Use explicit acceptance for ownership transfers, independent verification and runbooks for emergency action.</p></article><article><h3>Publish voting instructions and results</h3><p>Publish hub/gauge intentions and final ballots, validator-mandate rules and material voting-power holders at entity level. Preserve weighted dissent and avoid interpreting delegation as ownership.</p></article><article><h3>Record each payment’s approval and acceptance</h3><p>Keep the budget, recipient, declared conflicts, promised work, acceptance, entitlement, claim and valuation together. Independently reconcile recurring expenses and related-party OTC execution.</p></article><article><h3>Review and respond</h3><p>Commission periodic independent control review with access to off-chain agreements. Give affected stakeholders a right of response and publish corrections against specific evidence.</p></article></div>{source_list([('https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/g20-oecd-principles-of-corporate-governance-2023_ed750b30-en/full-report/component-8.html', 'G20/OECD Principles: board responsibilities'), ('https://legalinstruments.oecd.org/public/doc/151/body-text.en.html', 'OECD disclosure principles'), ('https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/2005/08/managing-conflict-of-interest-in-the-public-sector_g1gh5807.html', 'OECD conflict-of-interest toolkit'), ('https://www.iso.org/standard/65036.html', 'ISO 37000:2021 governance of organizations'), ('https://controlsframework.net/icif/index.php', 'COSO Internal Control—Integrated Framework'), ('https://aisel.aisnet.org/jais/vol19/iss10/1/', 'Beck et al.: blockchain governance research'), ('https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.12125', 'Hidden Shortcomings of DAOs'), ('https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/25741292.2023.2270220', 'Research on DAO governance impacts')])}</section>{nextlinks(('/treasury/', 'Back: treasury'), ('/evidence/', 'Next: evidence and method ' + ARROW))}</div>'''
rebalance = supplement['ceres_removal_rebalance']['facts']
authority_update = f'''<section id="allocation-use"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Ceres removal and redelegation</h2></div><ol class="timeline"><li><time datetime="2026-10-09T03:09:55Z">9 Oct · 03:09:55 UTC</time><div><h3>Ceres removed from the hub whitelist</h3><p>The published ERIS configuration address executed the removal.</p></div></li><li><time datetime="2026-10-09T03:10:35Z">9 Oct · 03:10:35 UTC</time><div><h3>Rebalance 40 seconds later</h3><p>Seven redelegations totalled {number(rebalance['total_LUNA'], 6)} LUNA, including {number(rebalance['from_removed_validator_LUNA'], 6)} LUNA away from Ceres.</p></div></li></ol><p>Compare the eligibility criteria, whitelist, gauge outputs and public rationale immediately before and after the change. These hub staking redelegations are distinct from Phoenix Alliance allocations. Timing alone does not establish retaliation, favoritism or coordinated voting.</p>{source_list([('https://chainsco.pe/terra2/tx/536298700377664639E3CF27AD0FED8495946C3F835FCEC64B02C97F6E15BA88', 'Removal transaction'), ('https://chainsco.pe/terra2/tx/42CF090DCD271A49A26E6679C9BA4597D47342C9603929D79F973D2AC12CE776', 'Rebalance transaction'), (LEADS + 'redelegations.json', 'All seven redelegations with event references')])}</section>
<section id="retained-roles"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Other retained permissions</h2></div><p>The 27 October queries show the published ERIS configuration address as hub and prop-gauge owner, while the hub’s raw vote operator points to the prop-gauge. Creda’s registry identifies the protocol fee account as reserve controller; the Portfolio’s migration administrator is a separate role.</p><p>The successful FEE_COLLECTOR assignment, Treasury configuration-role assignment and arbLUNA collateral addition also document uses of retained authority. These records show assigned permissions and asset movements; they do not establish personal profit. Two Creda reserve collections included 5,701.254836 and 2,679.750683 LUNA, alongside other assets that remain separately denominated.</p><p class="note">These current responses do not establish the requested historical snapshots: providers did not return headers confirming those heights. Match permissions to deployed code before asserting every supported capability, and do not infer exclusive personal key custody from a public role label.</p>{source_list([(LEADS + 'verified_findings.json', 'Authority events, current roles and reserve collections'), (LEADS + 'queries/hub_vote_operator_current.json', 'Raw hub vote-operator response'), (LEADS + 'queries/creda_roles_current.json', 'Creda reserve-controller roles')])}</section>
<section id="source-response"><div class="section-heading"><h2>Why ERIS says it made source code private</h2></div><p>ERIS’s retrospective says appropriate contracts were moved to private source for security. That is ERIS’s explanation for the missing public source.</p><p>The stated rationale does not independently establish stronger security or audit coverage for every deployed 2026 revision. Public audit reports exist; independent review still needs the auditor, scope, source commit, deployed checksum, outstanding findings and remediation.</p>{source_list([(YEAR_ARTICLE, 'ERIS explanation and security claims'), ('https://github.com/SCV-Security/PublicReports/tree/main/Eris%20Protocol', 'Published ERIS audit reports'), (LEADS + 'verified_findings.json', 'Scope of the transaction checks')])}</section>'''
governance = governance.replace('<section id="reforms">', authority_update + '<section id="reforms">', 1)
page('Governance and independent controls', governance, '/governance/')

# Evidence, provenance and correction boundaries.
evidence = head('Evidence and method', 'Voting histories, transaction responses, calculations and verification limits.') + f'''<div class="wrap page-content"><section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Voting-history downloads</h2></div>{table(['Dataset', 'Coverage', 'Download'], [['Phoenix board', 'A1–A289; all four directors', local('/record/audit/phoenix_board_votes_all.csv', 'CSV') + ' · ' + local('/record/audit/mb_voting_record.json', 'compiled record with per-ballot source locators')], ['Phoenix board, recent', 'A269–A289; last-four-weeks review window', local('/record/audit/phoenix_board_votes_last4weeks.csv', 'CSV')], ['ERIS fixed multisig', 'P1–P9; all five current voters', local('/record/audit/eris_multisig_votes_all.csv', 'CSV') + ' · ' + local('/record/audit/eris_multisig/proposals.json', 'proposal JSON')], ['Think Tank', 'P1–P5; all six current members', local('/record/audit/thinktank/votes.csv', 'CSV') + ' · ' + local('/record/audit/thinktank/proposals.json', 'proposal JSON')], ['Native 4853', '96 current ballots; 27 October, 15:07 UTC', local(REFRESH + 'votes.csv', 'CSV') + ' · ' + local(REFRESH + 'tally.json', 'tally JSON')], ['Lion DAO A33', 'Relationship, voting-power and timing analysis', local('/record/audit/lion_dao/ryan_relationship.json', 'JSON')]])}<p class="note">“All” means all records in the identified proposal range or capture—not every historical transaction on Terra. Current chain state can change after the snapshot. The <a href="/record/audit/native_4853_votes_all.csv">earlier 83-ballot native export</a> remains available for comparison.</p></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Evidence labels</h2></div><dl class="definitions"><dt>Confirmed record</dt><dd>A direct chain response, transaction, contract state, source-code line or dated primary document. A successful action proves use of authority, not exclusive key custody.</dd><dd></dd><dt>Derived analysis</dt><dd>Arithmetic, grouping or comparison of cited records. The full-hub-YES outcome and combined vAMP shares are scenarios, not observed events.</dd><dt>Interpretation</dt><dd>A relationship or pattern that justifies a question. Timing, payments, shared protocols and NFT collections alone do not establish ownership, coordination or motive.</dd><dt>Unresolved</dt><dd>Missing off-chain agreements, classification, historical membership or notification evidence. A search of these records cannot establish that no other evidence exists.</dd></dl></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Original records behind the supplied website</h2></div><p>The initial packet came from <a href="https://philipp-payments.pages.dev/">philipp-payments.pages.dev</a>. Its source index and embedded locators were used to identify original chain, forum and GitHub records where possible. Preserved source copies remain under <a href="/record/">/record/</a>; their original commentary is not adopted as this review’s conclusion.</p><ul><li>The compiled A1–A289 record retains per-proposal ballot query URLs, transaction hashes and source hashes.</li><li>The packaged authority ledger retains operation-level transaction locators. The original handover lines and current roles were checked separately.</li><li>Every retained data artifact has a sibling <code>.source.md</code> describing immediate source, upstream source where established, transformation and SHA-256.</li><li>The supplied uncorroborated spreadsheet is not published or used as an attribution source.</li></ul>{source_list([('https://github.com/PFC-Validator/phoenix-forensics', 'Audit repository and provenance register'), ('https://github.com/phoenix-directive/docs', 'Phoenix Directive original governance documents'), ('https://github.com/erisprotocol/contracts-tokenfactory', 'ERIS contracts and voting source'), ('https://forum.phoenix.money/', 'Phoenix governance forum'), ('https://phoenix.money/', 'Phoenix project website')])}</section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Scope and limitations</h2></div><ul><li>Historical governance review cutoff: 26 October 2026; native tally and scenario refreshed 27 October, approximately 15:07 UTC. Proposal 4853 remains open until 31 October at 03:40 UTC.</li><li>Source independence: the earlier HighStakes LCD is operated by a reviewed validator with an ABSTAIN ballot and approximately 3.254M LUNA of hub delegation. The 27 October refresh uses tdrsys; sequential queries do not establish a common block height. Hub and ICS20 configuration were exactly cross-checked against tdrsys.</li><li>Smart Stake validator-total weights are secondary analytics. Primary native ballots contain options, not per-ballot LUNA weights.</li><li>Not every packaged historical operation was independently replayed; that limitation is stated wherever the ledger is used.</li><li>Some providers omit older records or do not index all transactions. An empty search result cannot establish that an action never occurred.</li><li>Payment coverage and historical valuation vary by category. Gross flows are not net worth, profit or loss.</li><li>No identity research, surname-based searches or personal-contact calls are part of this review.</li></ul></section>
<section><div class="section-heading"><h2>Corrections and responses</h2></div><p>Corrections should identify the page, statement and supporting source. Responses and corrections should appear beside the affected claim.</p><button class="button secondary" data-share>Copy this page’s link</button><p class="share-status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p></section>{nextlinks(('/governance/', 'Back: governance reforms'), ('/', 'Return to overview ' + ARROW))}</div>'''
evidence_update = f'''<section id="transaction-supplement"><div class="section-heading"><h2>27 October transaction supplement</h2><p>48 successful primary transaction records, with explicit limits on what each proves.</p></div><p>For all 38 Terra transactions, SHA-256 recomputed from the original encoded transaction bytes matches the cited hash; RPC height and execution code also match the LCD response. The archive endpoints may share infrastructure. This is a byte/hash and execution consistency check, not independent consensus or inclusion-proof verification.</p><p>The supplement contains 466 transfer rows, eight funded voting locks, seven redelegations and 23 findings with stated verification limits. Native transfers, CW20 and ERC20 movements retain their units. Fee-share events annotate bank transfers rather than adding the same receipt again; eight empty-amount transfer events remain explicitly unquantified.</p>{table(['Evidence', 'Download', 'Boundary'], [['Findings and lead status', local(LEADS + 'verified_findings.json', 'Findings') + ' · ' + local(LEADS + 'lead_status.json', 'Status by lead'), 'A supported finding may verify only part of a lead'], ['Transfers', local(LEADS + 'transfers.csv', 'CSV') + ' · ' + local(LEADS + 'transfers.json', 'JSON'), 'Movements are not automatically income'], ['Funding and current Creda positions', local(LEADS + 'year_review_analysis.json', 'Calculations'), 'External Votion payment is funder-reported; position shares are approximate'], ['Primary captures and failed attempts', local(LEADS + 'capture_manifest.json', 'Transaction manifest') + ' · ' + local(LEADS + 'query_manifest.json', 'Query manifest'), 'Six cited transactions remain unavailable; absence is not established'], ['Original-byte consistency checks', local(LEADS + 'rpc_manifest.json', 'RPC manifest'), 'No independent consensus verification asserted']])}<p>Fourteen requested historical state queries lacked response headers confirming the requested heights. Separate current captures do not replace them. Full cumulative fee histories, lock changes, historical validator eligibility and cross-chain message/nonce reconciliation remain open.</p><p class="note">The native tally and YES scenario were updated on 27 October; earlier voting records are retained separately. This review does not independently audit every transaction.</p>{source_list([(LEADS, 'Browse supplemental datasets'), (LEADS + 'year_review_analysis.json.source.md', 'Funding-analysis provenance'), (YEAR_ARTICLE, 'ERIS retrospective and stated response')])}</section>'''
evidence = evidence.replace('Evidence cutoff: 26 October 2026;', 'Core voting snapshot: 26 October 2026;', 1)
evidence = insert_before_reading(evidence, evidence_update)
